There are $20$ even numbers, $12$ numbers less than $13$, and $6$ even numbers less than $13$. Place the overlap first, then subtract to find the other regions.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (7,4);
\draw (2.6,2) circle (1.5);
\draw (4.4,2) circle (1.5);
\node at (0.35,3.65) {$S$};
\node at (1.8,3) {$A$};
\node at (5.2,3) {$B$};
\node at (1.8,1.9) {$14$};
\node at (3.5,2) {$6$};
\node at (5.2,1.9) {$6$};
\node at (6.35,0.45) {$14$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

(a)

$\begin{aligned}P(A\cup B)&=P(A)+P(B)-P(A\cap B)\\&=\frac{20}{40}+\frac{12}{40}-\frac{6}{40}\\&=\frac{26}{40}\\&=\frac{13}{20}\end{aligned}$

(b) Neither event is the complement of the union.

$\begin{aligned}P(A'\cap B')&=1-P(A\cup B)\\&=1-\frac{13}{20}\\&=\frac{7}{20}\end{aligned}$

A single counter has only one colour, so the colour events are mutually exclusive. There are $20$ counters altogether.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (8,3.1);
\draw (1.5,1.65) circle (0.9);
\draw (4,1.65) circle (0.9);
\draw (6.5,1.65) circle (0.9);
\node at (0.3,2.8) {$S$};
\node at (1.5,2) {$R$};
\node at (1.5,1.3) {$5$};
\node at (4,2) {$G$};
\node at (4,1.3) {$4$};
\node at (6.5,2) {$B$};
\node at (6.5,1.3) {$3$};
\node at (4,0.35) {Yellow: $8$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

(a)

$\begin{aligned}P(R\cup G\cup B)&=P(R)+P(G)+P(B)\\&=\frac{5}{20}+\frac{4}{20}+\frac{3}{20}\\&=\frac{3}{5}\end{aligned}$

(b)

$\begin{aligned}P(Y)&=1-P(R\cup G\cup B)\\&=1-\frac{3}{5}\\&=\frac{2}{5}\end{aligned}$

(a) Rearrange the addition rule to find the overlap.

$\begin{aligned}P(A\cap B)&=P(A)+P(B)-P(A\cup B)\\&=0.48+0.37-0.70\\&=0.15\end{aligned}$

The remaining regions follow by subtraction.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (7,4);
\draw (2.6,2) circle (1.5);
\draw (4.4,2) circle (1.5);
\node at (0.35,3.65) {$S$};
\node at (1.8,3) {$A$};
\node at (5.2,3) {$B$};
\node at (1.8,1.9) {$0.33$};
\node at (3.5,2) {$0.15$};
\node at (5.2,1.9) {$0.22$};
\node at (6.35,0.45) {$0.30$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

(b) $A'\cap B$ is the part of $B$ outside the overlap.

$\begin{aligned}P(A'\cap B)&=P(B)-P(A\cap B)\\&=0.37-0.15\\&=0.22\end{aligned}$

Let $O$ denote an oil change and $F$ a filter change. Remove the overlap from $O$ to find $O$ only. The shaded region is the required event; the other probabilities are unspecified.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{requiredFill}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\begin{scope}
\clip (2.6,2) circle (1.5);
\fill[requiredFill] (0,0) rectangle (7,4);
\fill[white] (4.4,2) circle (1.5);
\end{scope}
\draw (0,0) rectangle (7,4);
\draw (2.6,2) circle (1.5);
\draw (4.4,2) circle (1.5);
\node at (0.35,3.65) {$S$};
\node at (1.8,3) {$O$};
\node at (5.2,3) {$F$};
\node at (1.8,1.9) {$\frac{1}{2}$};
\node at (3.5,2) {$\frac{1}{5}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{aligned}P(O\cap F')&=P(O)-P(O\cap F)\\&=\frac{7}{10}-\frac{1}{5}\\&=\frac{5}{10}\\&=\frac{1}{2}\end{aligned}$

Subtract the overlap from $A$ to find $A$ only.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (8,4.4);
\draw (3,2.2) circle (1.7);
\draw (5,2.2) circle (1.7);
\node at (0.35,4.05) {$S$};
\node at (2,3.3) {$A$};
\node at (6,3.3) {$B$};
\node at (2,2) {$\frac{m-1}{30}$};
\node at (4,2.2) {$\frac{1}{30}$};
\node at (6,2) {$\frac{n-1}{30}$};
\node at (7.3,0.45) {$\frac{1}{30}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{aligned}P(A\cap B')&=P(A)-P(A\cap B)\\&=\frac{m}{m+n}-\frac{1}{m+n}\\&=\frac{m-1}{30}\end{aligned}$

Use the given probability to find $m$, then use the total to find $n$.

$\begin{aligned}\frac{m-1}{30}&=0.40\\m-1&=12\\m&=13\end{aligned}$

$\begin{aligned}n&=30-13\\&=17\end{aligned}$

At least one event does not occur exactly when it is not the case that both events occur. Thus $A'\cup B'$ is the complement of $A\cap B$. The shaded regions show this complement.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1cm,y=1cm,draw=black,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{requiredFill}{HTML}{D4E8FF}
\fill[requiredFill] (0,0) rectangle (7,4);
\begin{scope}
\clip (2.6,2) circle (1.5);
\fill[white] (4.4,2) circle (1.5);
\end{scope}
\draw (0,0) rectangle (7,4);
\draw (2.6,2) circle (1.5);
\draw (4.4,2) circle (1.5);
\node at (0.35,3.65) {$S$};
\node at (1.8,3) {$A$};
\node at (5.2,3) {$B$};
\node at (1.8,1.9) {$\frac{3}{10}$};
\node at (3.5,2) {$\frac{3}{10}$};
\node at (5.2,1.9) {$\frac{1}{5}$};
\node at (6.35,0.45) {$\frac{1}{5}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$\begin{aligned}P(A'\cup B')&=1-P(A\cap B)\\&=1-\frac{3}{10}\\&=\frac{7}{10}\end{aligned}$
